- cxx-mixed-designated-initializer: quickjs.h's JSCFunctionListEntry-building
  macros (JS_CFUNC_DEF and the 15 others alongside it) each build a
  brace-enclosed initializer that mixes positional clauses (name, prop_flags,
  def_type, magic) with a designated clause (.u = {...}) in the same
  brace-enclosed list. C allows that mix; C++ (P1975/P0329) requires a list
  to be either fully positional or fully designated, so g++/clang++ reject
  every one of these macros with "either all initializer clauses should be
  designated or none of them should be". Confirmed under g++ -std=c++20 on
  both quickjs-2026-06-04 and quickjs-2025-09-13. Fixed via
  patches/004-cxx-designated.patch (designates all five outer fields; the
  nested .u = {...} content is left positional since it's internally
  consistent on its own).

    // any use of a JS_CFUNC_DEF-family macro in a .cpp translation unit,
    // e.g. quickjs-libc.c's JSCFunctionListEntry tables compiled as C++:
    static const JSCFunctionListEntry proto_funcs[] = {
        JS_CFUNC_DEF("foo", 0, js_foo),
    };
    // g++ -std=c++20: error: either all initializer clauses should be
    // designated or none of them should be

- config-debugger-always-required: the CMake option CONFIG_DEBUGGER (default
  ON, but was OFF for WASI/EMSCRIPTEN builds before this fix) controls only
  whether defs.cmake adds quickjs-debugger.c and
  quickjs-debugger-transport-*.c to QUICKJS_SOURCES. quickjs.c itself calls
  js_debugger_check(), js_debugger_exception(), js_debugger_free(),
  js_debugger_free_context() and js_debugger_new_context() unconditionally
  (no #ifdef CONFIG_DEBUGGER around the call sites in quickjs.c), so
  building with CONFIG_DEBUGGER=OFF always fails at link time with undefined
  symbols, on every platform, not just wasm. Found while adding Emscripten
  support: CMakeLists.txt defaulted CONFIG_DEBUGGER to OFF for
  WASI/EMSCRIPTEN, which broke the link; changed the wasm-target default
  back to ON as a build-system-level workaround (see CMakeLists.txt), but
  the root cause (missing #ifdef guards in quickjs.c) is still present and
  CONFIG_DEBUGGER=OFF remains broken for every target, including native.

    cmake -B build -DCONFIG_DEBUGGER=OFF && cmake --build build --target qjs
    # wasm-ld/ld: undefined symbol: js_debugger_check (and 4 related symbols)

- missing-line-number-single-line-function: a stack frame for a function
  whose entire body (declaration through the throwing statement) sits on
  one source line prints with no `:<line>` at all - just the bare
  filename - while every frame for code on its own line gets a correct
  line number. Root cause: find_line_num() (quickjs.c:6425) treats
  `!b->debug.pc2line_buf` as "function was stripped, no debug info" and
  returns -1 (quickjs.c:6431-6433) without ever distinguishing that case
  from "debug info present, but this function's whole bytecode maps to
  one unchanging line so the pc2line delta stream is legitimately empty".
  At bytecode-generation time (quickjs.c:32798)
  `b->debug.pc2line_buf = js_realloc(ctx, fd->pc2line.buf, fd->pc2line.size)`
  and js_realloc(ctx, ptr, 0) is documented to return NULL for a
  zero-size request (quickjs.c:1385-1392, `!ret && size != 0` explicitly
  excludes size==0 from being treated as an OOM failure) - so a one-line
  function ends up with has_debug=1 but pc2line_buf=NULL, and
  find_line_num() silently drops the line instead of falling back to
  b->debug.line_num (the function's own declared line, sitting right
  there on the same struct). Splitting the identical code across multiple
  lines makes the pc2line stream non-empty and the bug disappears. Found
  while writing an exception/stack-trace test suite for qjs-modules
  (tests/test_exceptions.js there documents this as a known-failing case).

    function foo() { throw new Error("boom"); }
    function bar() { foo(); }
    bar();
    // actual (foo/bar frames have no line number):
    //   at foo (script.js)
    //   at bar (script.js)
    //   at <anonymous> (script.js:3)
    // expected: every frame carries a `:<line>`, e.g. "at foo (script.js:1)"

- no-column-number-tracking: quickjs.c never tracks source column
  positions anywhere - not in the parser's token/error reporting, not in
  the per-function pc2line debug stream (there is no pc2col equivalent),
  and not on thrown Error objects (no `.columnNumber`, and `.stack`
  frames never carry a column, only a line). The only place a column
  even appears is js_debugger_current_location() (quickjs.c:54678-54697),
  which fills in a JSDebuggerLocation struct that has a `column` field,
  but the code hardcodes it: `location.column = 0;` with the comment
  "quickjs has no column info." This isn't a case of column info being
  tracked somewhere and just not surfaced - there is no source of truth
  for it anywhere in the engine to surface. Confirmed by grepping
  quickjs.c for any column/col-related field or variable name: none
  exist outside that one hardcoded 0.

    try { null.foo; } catch(e) { console.log(e.stack, e.columnNumber); }
    // actual: e.stack has a line number but no column; e.columnNumber
    //         is undefined
    // expected (for parity with V8/JSC): a column pointing at `.foo`

- quickjs-install-prefix-always-empty (CMakeLists.txt:684): QUICKJS_INSTALL_PREFIX
  is only ever assigned on a commented-out line (684, "#set(QUICKJS_INSTALL_PREFIX
  ...)"), so every other use of it - the RPATH/INSTALL_RPATH properties (lines
  731-732, 819-820, 841-843) and the QUICKJS_PREFIX compile definitions baked
  into the qjs/qjsc targets (lines 697, 718, 825, 848) - always evaluates to an
  empty string, regardless of CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX or any user setting. Found
  while fixing the related QUICKJS_PREFIX cache-variable bug just above it in
  the file; not fixed here since it's unclear whether these call sites were
  meant to reference QUICKJS_INSTALL_PREFIX (a distinct, never-populated
  variable) or the QUICKJS_PREFIX cache variable instead.

    cmake -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/opt/quickjs && cmake --build build
    # binaries end up with RPATH "/lib" (not "/opt/quickjs/lib") and
    # QUICKJS_PREFIX="" baked in as a compile definition

